MUTATION ARM for `test_matrix_d6_skip_discipline.py`, the branch that closes the
four `test_flow_matrix_coverage.py` reds.

RE-PLANTS: `_build_probes` reports one completed-work checkpoint per COMPLETED
PROBE instead of per SCENARIO — the pre-fix granularity. The supervisor's lease
is armed when the item starts and nothing renews it until the first checkpoint,
so at probe granularity that interval is one whole probe (three to five
`flow_compliance_check` subprocesses). MEASURED on live main 72bd2679 at
`--cpus=4`, whole file, progress stream kept: 69 checkpoints over 693.1 s, and
exactly ONE gap over the nested outcome run's 60 s lease — the FIRST one,
`collection_finish -> [1/69]` at 60.873 s.

WHY THIS PATCH DID NOT EXIST BEFORE, stated because the lander's refusal was
about its absence and not about the tests. The branch shipped no
`tools/ci/mutation_arm/*.patch` of its own. `falsref.sh` globs the WHOLE
directory (`MUTS=$(ls $D/G/tools/ci/mutation_arm/*.patch)`), so it inherited the
five patches already on main — `issue1446_*`, `issue1970_*` x2,
`signoff-producer-*` x2 — applied all five (all five DO apply; verified), ran
this branch's two files, and they stayed green. They stayed green correctly:
none of those five has anything to do with D6 probe granularity. The arm was
measuring other branches' defects.

MEASURED here, on tree R = live main + this branch's two test files:

    without this patch   12 passed                                    (tree G)
    with this patch      1 failed, 11 passed                          (tree M)
    FAILED test_the_first_checkpoint_costs_one_SCENARIO_not_one_whole_probe
      AssertionError: no checkpoint was reported while both probes were still
      inside their first scenario — the first renewal costs a whole probe,
      which is the 60.873 s gap this test exists to forbid

`test_d6_completed_probe_progress_is_not_blocked_by_input_order` stays green
under it, correctly: its property is that a LATER completion is not withheld
behind an earlier input, and this defect is about the FIRST one.

